--- Case A: module 4 ("QA Single-Store Grocery"), zone 3 — exactly one
    active store (id=59), used because it isolates single_store_id +
    open_now_stores_count on the SAME store, proving AC2 (single_store_id is
    schedule-independent) alongside AC1 in one before/after pair. ---

BASELINE (store 59's store_schedule day=0 row: 00:00:00–23:59:00, i.e. open
right now):
  GET /api/v1/module   Header: zoneId: [3]
  -> module id=4 "QA Single-Store Grocery":
     stores_count: 1
     active_stores_count: 1
     open_now_stores_count: 1
     single_store_id: 59

MUTATION (clone DB only):
  UPDATE store_schedule SET closing_time='03:00:00'
    WHERE store_id=59 AND day=0;
  -- store 59 is now schedule-closed as of ~20:43 (closing_time 03:00 < now).

AFTER (same request, store 59 now schedule-closed for today):
  GET /api/v1/module   Header: zoneId: [3]
  -> module id=4 "QA Single-Store Grocery":
     stores_count: 1
     active_stores_count: 1        <- UNCHANGED (AC2: active/listable status untouched by schedule)
     open_now_stores_count: 0      <- AC1: correctly reflects schedule-closed
     single_store_id: 59           <- UNCHANGED (AC2: resolution stays keyed off active_stores_count only)

Reverted (clone DB only): closing_time restored to 23:59:00; re-fetch showed
open_now_stores_count back to 1, active_stores_count/single_store_id
unchanged throughout — confirms the field tracks live schedule state, not a
one-way/cached flag.

--- Case B: module 1 ("Grocery & Food"), zone 2 (Abu Dhabi, 19 stores,
    14 active) — multi-store module, exercises the aggregate COUNT path. ---

BASELINE (store id=8 "Abu Dhabi Fresh Market", schedule day=0: 08:00–23:00,
open now):
  GET /api/v1/module   Header: zoneId: [2]
  -> module id=1: active_stores_count=14, open_now_stores_count=14

MUTATION (clone DB only): UPDATE store_schedule SET closing_time='12:00:00'
  WHERE store_id=8 AND day=0;  (store 8 now schedule-closed at ~20:4x)

AFTER:
  GET /api/v1/module   Header: zoneId: [2]
  -> module id=1: active_stores_count=14 (unchanged), open_now_stores_count=13
     (exactly one fewer — the schedule-closed store, matching a raw SQL
     cross-check: `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM stores s JOIN store_schedule ss ...
     WHERE ss.day=DAYOFWEEK(NOW())-1 AND ss.opening_time<TIME(NOW())
     AND ss.closing_time>TIME(NOW())` independently returned 13 ids)

Reverted (clone DB only): closing_time restored to 23:00:00; re-fetch showed
open_now_stores_count back to 14 == active_stores_count.

